{"title":"Enlightenment ideas and the belief in progress leading up to the Industrial Revolution","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/929072009/0/marginalrevolution~Enlightenment-ideas-and-the-belief-in-progress-leading-up-to-the-Industrial-Revolution.html","date":1763885756000,"content":"<blockquote><p>Using textual analysis of 173,031 works printed in England between 1500 and 1900, we test whether British culture evolved to manifest a heightened belief in progress associated with science and industry. Our analysis yields three main findings. First, there was a separation in the language of science and religion beginning in the 17th century. Second, scientific volumes became more progress-oriented during the Enlightenment. Third, industrial works—especially those at the science-political economy nexus—were more progress-oriented beginning in the 17th century. It was therefore the more pragmatic, industrial works which reflected the cultural values cited as important for Britain’s takeoff.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>That is from <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/esi_working_papers/393/\" target=\"_blank\">a paper</a> by Ali Almelhem, Murat Iyigun, Austin Kennedy, and Jared Rubin.  Now <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://x.com/jaredcrubin/status/1991181232164745481\" target=\"_blank\">forthcoming at the QJE</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/11/enlightenment-ideas-and-the-belief-in-progress-leading-up-to-the-industrial-revolution.html\">Enlightenment ideas and the belief in progress leading up to the Industrial Revolution</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/929072009/0/margina